;; talk/private--protocol.scm — objections to surfaces/private.scm, lens: protocol.

(objection 'o-private-protocol-1 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'private-home :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/balance/set-credit-limit.ts"
    "The home screen shows can-send beside the answer to 'how much is mine for sure', and can-send includes unused credit the company granted the person, which the protocol defines as partly borrowed. The company sets that credit line by proposing set_credit_limit; the handler applies it with no step where the person accepts. So the company can raise the person's spendable figure without being asked, the person can pay with it believing it is their money, and what they spent becomes a debt to the company. On leaving, a debt beyond locked money is taken from their money on the chain and the rest stays recorded against later arrivals. The surface declares borrowing a gap and never shows credit-granted-to-me, so the person sees borrowed room as spendable money and is never told they can be put in debt by the other side."))

(objection 'o-private-protocol-2 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'private-default-auto-top-up :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/rebalance/request-collateral.ts"
    "d-private-consent says to ask before a signature that spends a fee and to sign silently only what adds to the person's side. This default pays a fee silently, up to 15 in the seeded policy. The collateral request debits the fee from the person's side at the moment of asking, before the company has locked anything, and it comes back only if the company sends it back. The default is also labelled as dormant while the allowed amount is below 500, but the person can raise the allowed amount at the first payment, and from then on the fee is spent with no question and no record the person was shown. A person who set a larger allowance to receive one payment pays the fee unasked, repeatedly if the company never locks. The surface's own rule and its own default contradict each other, and the cost lands on the person."))

(objection 'o-private-protocol-3 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'd-private-evidence :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/watchtower/dispute-watch.ts"
    "The leaning answers 'if I lose this phone, can I still get my money back' with a service that 'wakes the person'. The watch code states it is detection and notify only: it sends a push to a registered device and never answers the dispute. With the phone lost or broken, the push goes to the lost device, nobody answers inside the response window, and the older state the other side put on the chain can stand. The protocol has a separate last-resort appointment in which the watching service holds a narrow authorization to answer a dispute itself (core/watchtower/last-resort-appointment.ts); the decision does not list it as an option and never says that without it a wake cannot protect a person who has lost the phone. It also asks the person to answer within hours, which the person's own cannot-list rules out."))

(objection 'o-private-protocol-4 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'd-private-first-screen :lens "protocol"
  :claim (canon 'keep-evidence
    "The first screen calls the locked share 'mine for sure' and ties that to nothing. The protocol makes it certain only while the person (or someone appointed) holds the latest state both sides signed and answers inside the window; nobody else has to supply it. The home screen shows no sign of whether the last copy was accepted by the watching service, which the surface lists as a need and then puts only on a screen that shows nothing. So after a failed copy or a missed answer the screen still says 'for sure' about money the person can lose, which is the worst failure for this surface: a safety claim the protocol does not back at that moment."))

(objection 'o-private-protocol-5 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'private-default-pay-speed :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/htlc/direct.ts"
    "Instant mode makes a routed payment claimable for about two minutes; it completes only when the recipient's side reveals the secret in that time, and the payer cannot take it back earlier (payer-cannot-recall). The surface picks instant for every payment so the payer's money returns quickly, and says nothing about the person on the other end, who by the surface's own description is often a phone that is asleep. For the get-paid job this means a payment to a sleeping phone expires and the payer is told it failed, while the person who was meant to be paid never learns anything arrived. The job 'let someone pay me' depends on the recipient's runtime being live in a window of minutes; nothing in the get-paid flow or the pay-handle need says so."))

(objection 'o-private-protocol-6 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'private-default-claim-incoming :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/htlc/direct.ts"
    "The default is labelled risk-on nobody. Claiming a payment moves the held amount to the person's side, and when the company's collateral does not cover it, it lands as money the company owes with nothing locked, up to the allowance the person gave. That is risk the person carries, so the label should be holder, as it is on the other defaults that touch the allowance. Labelled nobody, it is excluded from every list of silent risk the person is told about at joining."))
